use std
into meshcmp.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its `main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with mc_ is exported). It measures the shape of one triangle mesh so Amni-CAD can compare a design with a scanned or modeled rival product: size along the mesh's own principal axes, surface area, volume, how boxy, flat or softly curved the surfaces are, and the silhouette seen along any direction. Lengths are millimeters.

```amni
extern fn mc_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "mc_view_f64";
```

Module state. Growable f64 arrays: `T` (9 per triangle: x, y, z of its three corners), `R` (64 results), `Q` (one per cell of the silhouette grid, 1 when covered).

`R` layout. Filled by `mc_stats`: 0 surface area; 1 volume (absolute value of the signed volume); 2, 3, 4 area-weighted centroid x, y, z; 5, 6, 7 extents along principal axes 1, 2, 3 (largest spread first); 8 to 16 the three principal axes as unit vectors, 3 entries each, axis 1 first; 17 boxy share: share of area whose unit normal is within 10 degrees of plus or minus one of the three principal axes; 18 flat share: share of area whose unit normal is within 10 degrees of plus or minus principal axis 3; 19 compactness: 36 pi times volume squared over area cubed; 20 soft share: share of area whose unit normal is more than 25 degrees from plus or minus every principal axis; 21, 22, 23 extents along x, y, z. Filled by `mc_proj`: 30 silhouette area; 31 silhouette perimeter (marching squares outline); 32 roundness: 4 pi times silhouette area over perimeter squared; 33 covered cells; 34 silhouette extent along u; 35 silhouette extent along v.

Sizing helper. `zf(xs, n, x)` takes an f64 growable array by reference: xs is emptied, then gets `reserve`(n), then is resized to n entries equal to x.

The exported function `mc_views()` registers every array JavaScript reads or writes through mc_view_f64: 1 `T`, 2 `R`, 3 `Q`. It returns 0.

The exported function `mc_setup(n)` (an i32 triangle count) sizes `T` to 9 times n zeros and `R` to 64 zeros with `zf`, sizes `Q` to 1 zero, calls `mc_views` and returns 0. JavaScript then fills `T` through its view.

`jacobi(c)` takes a growable f64 array c of 18 entries by reference: entries 0 to 8 hold a symmetric 3 by 3 matrix row by row (entry 3 i plus j is row i, column j). It runs cyclic Jacobi rotations: up to 50 sweeps over the pairs (0, 1), (0, 2), (1, 2); a sweep ends early when the sum of squares of the three off-diagonal entries is at most 1e-24 times (1 plus the sum of squares of the diagonal). For a pair (p, q) whose entry is not 0: theta = (a_qq minus a_pp) over (2 a_pq), t = sign of theta (1 when theta is 0) over (absolute theta plus the square root of theta squared plus 1), cs = 1 over the square root of t squared plus 1, sn = t cs; the matrix becomes J transposed times A times J where J is the identity with J_pp = cs, J_qq = cs, J_pq = sn, J_qp = minus sn, and the eigenvector matrix V (entries 9 to 17, row by row, starting as the identity) becomes V times J. Afterwards the eigenvalues are the diagonal and column k of V is the eigenvector of diagonal entry k. It returns 0.

The exported function `mc_stats(n)` (an i32 triangle count) returns 0 after filling `R` entries 0 to 23. First pass over the triangles with corners a, b, c: cross = (b minus a) cross (c minus a), triangle area = half its length; add the area to `R` 0; add a dot cross over 6 to the signed volume; add the area times the triangle centroid to the centroid sums; add the second-moment sums: for each pair of axes (i, j) add area over 12 times (a_i a_j plus b_i b_j plus c_i c_j plus s_i s_j) where s = a plus b plus c; track the smallest and largest x, y, z of all corners. When the total area is 0 return 0. The centroid m is the centroid sums over the area; the covariance is the second-moment sums over the area minus m_i m_j. Put the covariance into a fresh 18-entry array, run `jacobi`, order the three eigenvalues from largest to smallest and take the matching columns of V, each scaled to unit length, as principal axes 1, 2, 3 into `R` 8 to 16. Second pass: project every corner on each principal axis and keep the smallest and largest per axis, giving the extents `R` 5, 6, 7; for each triangle with area above 0 compute the unit normal and the absolute dot products with the three axes; when the largest of the three is at least cos 10 degrees (0.98480775) add its area to the boxy sum; when the one with axis 3 is at least 0.98480775 add its area to the flat sum; when the largest is at most cos 25 degrees (0.90630779) add its area to the soft sum. `R` 1 is the absolute value of the signed volume, `R` 17, 18 and 20 are those sums over the total area, `R` 19 is 36 pi times `R` 1 squared over `R` 0 cubed, and `R` 21, 22, 23 are the x, y, z extents.

The exported function `mc_proj(n, dx, dy, dz, res)` (an i32 triangle count, three reals for the view direction and an i32 grid resolution) returns the i32 number of covered cells after filling `R` 30 to 35 with the silhouette of the mesh seen along the direction d. d is (dx, dy, dz) scaled to unit length. up is (1, 0, 0) when the absolute value of d's z is above 0.9, else (0, 0, 1); u = up cross d scaled to unit length; v = d cross u. Project every corner to (p dot u, p dot v) and find the smallest and largest of each; the cell size h is the larger extent over res (1e-9 when that is 0); the grid starts one cell before the smallest values, nu = 3 plus the floor of the u extent over h, nv = 3 plus the floor of the v extent over h; `Q` is sized with `zf` to nu times nv zeros. For each triangle with projected corners p0, p1, p2 and twice-signed area w = (p1 minus p0) cross (p2 minus p0) in 2D: skip it when the absolute value of w is below 1e-12; for every cell whose center lies in the triangle's projected box (cell i from the floor of (its smallest u minus the grid start) over h to the floor of (its largest u minus the start) over h, the same for j along v, both limited to the grid) test the center c with the three edge values e0 = (p1 minus p0) cross (c minus p0), e1 = (p2 minus p1) cross (c minus p1), e2 = (p0 minus p2) cross (c minus p2), each multiplied by the sign of w; when all three are at least 0 set `Q`[i plus nu times j] to 1. Then count covered cells, and measure the outline with marching squares: for every block of 2 by 2 neighboring cells (i and i plus 1, j and j plus 1, i from 0 to nu minus 2, j from 0 to nv minus 2) count how many of its four cells are covered and whether they are diagonal: 1 or 3 covered add h times 0.70710678 (one diagonal cut), 2 covered side by side (sharing a side) add h, 2 covered on a diagonal add h times 1.41421356 (two cuts), 0 or 4 add nothing. `R` 30 = covered times h squared, `R` 31 = that outline length, `R` 32 = 4 pi `R` 30 over `R` 31 squared (0 when `R` 31 is 0), `R` 33 = covered, `R` 34 and `R` 35 the u and v extents of the projected corners.

`main()` returns 0.
